insert into public.scripture_did_you_know(reference,category,fact,source_note) values
('Judges 7:19','plain_language','What is the ''middle watch'' in today''s language? Think of the middle overnight security shift. In this ancient three-watch system, it was roughly 10 PM to 2 AM. Gideon arrived just as guards changed shifts—an especially confusing moment for a sleeping camp.','Judges 7:19; OTE 28(3) (2015), https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/ote/v28n3/10.pdf ; exact hours varied by season and watch system.'),
('Judges 7:18','ancient_culture','A trumpet here was not like the brass instrument in a modern marching band. It was a horn used to send a loud signal across a camp. Gideon''s three groups could coordinate the moment everyone shouted without a phone or radio.','Judges 7:16–20; historical shofar/trumpet signaling; archaeological context, ancient Near East.'),
('Judges 7:20','plain_language','A ''pitcher'' in older translations means a jar or clay container, not necessarily the plastic jug you might use today. The fighters broke their jars to expose burning torches, making sudden light appear around the enemy.','Judges 7:16–20; compare KJV ''pitchers'' with modern ''jars'' translations.'),
('Exodus 14:24','plain_language','The ''morning watch'' was the last shift before sunrise. In the earlier three-watch way of dividing the night, it roughly covered 2 AM until morning. The Egyptian army was thrown into confusion before the day fully began.','Exodus 14:24; study of ancient Israelite night watches, https://www.scielo.org.za/pdf/ote/v28n3/10.pdf.'),
('Matthew 14:25','plain_language','The ''fourth watch of the night'' sounds mysterious, but it meant the final overnight shift—roughly 3 AM to 6 AM in the four-watch system familiar in the Roman period. Jesus approached the boat near dawn, when the disciples had already spent hours battling the wind.','Matthew 14:24–25; Roman-period four-watch division, note seasonal variation.'),
('Mark 13:35','ancient_culture','Jesus names four parts of the night: evening, midnight, cockcrow, and morning. They sound like four alarm settings; in Roman-era practice they were the night''s guard shifts. His point is to stay alert even when the timing is unknown.','Mark 13:35; Greek terms and Roman-era night watches.'),
('Luke 2:7','scholarly_debate','Was Jesus born in a busy commercial inn? Scholars debate this familiar picture. The Greek word katalyma can mean a guest room or lodging place, and Luke uses it for an upstairs guest room elsewhere. The verse does not describe an innkeeper refusing them.','Luke 2:7; Luke 22:11; Greek κατάλυμα; compare Luke 10:34''s distinct term for an inn.'),
('Luke 2:8','ancient_culture','Shepherds were working outside at night, keeping watch over their flock. That is why the angelic announcement meets people doing an ordinary night job, not an audience gathered for a holiday service.','Luke 2:8–12.'),
('Matthew 2:1','scholarly_agreement','How many wise men visited Jesus? Matthew never gives a number! It lists three kinds of gifts—gold, frankincense, and myrrh—but not three visitors. The familiar picture of exactly three is tradition, not a headcount in the chapter.','Matthew 2:1–11; compare verses 1 and 11.'),
('Matthew 2:11','plain_language','The visitors from the east came to a ''house,'' according to Matthew—not to a stable in this verse. That helps explain why scholars and artists distinguish their visit from Luke''s account of the newborn Jesus laid in a manger.','Matthew 2:11; Luke 2:7; chronology reconstructed from two distinct accounts.'),
('Numbers 6:24','earliest_evidence','Tiny silver rolls discovered near Jerusalem in 1979 preserve wording close to this blessing. Dated roughly to the late 600s or early 500s BC, they are among the earliest surviving physical witnesses to words found in the Bible. They are not a whole book.','Numbers 6:24–26; Ketef Hinnom silver amulets, Tel Aviv University scholarship https://cris.tau.ac.il/en/publications/a-new-appraisal-of-the-silver-amulets-from-ketef-hinnom/.'),
('Numbers 6:25','earliest_evidence','The ancient silver amulets containing this blessing were rolled up so small they could be worn. That means the biblical blessing was not only copied in long scrolls; people could carry words of blessing in tiny inscribed objects.','Numbers 6:24–26; Ketef Hinnom silver amulets, https://image-database.nes.lsa.umich.edu/items/show/96.'),
('Job 1:1','scholarly_debate','Was Job the first Bible book ever written? Nobody can demonstrate that. The story has an ancient setting, but the book gives no author''s name or writing date, and scholars propose several different centuries for its composition. ''Oldest story setting'' and ''earliest surviving written book'' are different questions.','Job 1:1; Cambridge Companion to Biblical Wisdom Literature (Job dating) and Bible Gateway overview, https://www.biblegateway.com/learn/bible-101/about-the-bible/when-was-the-bible-written/.'),
('Job 1:6','scholarly_debate','The Hebrew word often translated ''Satan'' in this scene has the sense of ''the accuser'' or ''the adversary.'' Scholars debate how the figure''s role in Job relates to later descriptions of Satan. Understanding that question helps readers avoid importing every later detail into this opening scene.','Job 1:6–12; Hebrew ha-satan; scholarly discussion of Job''s literary and theological context.'),
('Isaiah 1:1','earliest_evidence','In 1947, scrolls found near the Dead Sea included what became known as the Great Isaiah Scroll. It preserves almost the entire book of Isaiah in a manuscript roughly two thousand years old—far earlier than the complete medieval Hebrew Bibles used by many modern editions.','Great Isaiah Scroll 1QIsa-a; Israel Antiquities Authority, https://www.deadseascrolls.org.il/learn-about-the-scrolls/discovery-and-publication?locale=en_US ; Library of Congress https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/scrolls/intr.html.'),
('Isaiah 40:3','scholarly_debate','Was every part of Isaiah written at exactly the same time? Many scholars distinguish sections reflecting different historical periods, while traditional views emphasize the book''s unity under Isaiah. The debate concerns composition, not whether ancient Isaiah manuscripts exist.','Isaiah 1; 40; 56; scholarly literary composition and the Great Isaiah Scroll; distinction between textual witnesses and original authorship.'),
('John 1:1','original_language','John begins with ''the Word.'' The Greek is logos—a word that can describe speech, message, or reason. John is not saying Jesus was merely a sentence on a page; he uses this familiar term to introduce the One through whom everything was made.','John 1:1–3,14; Greek λόγος; compare Genesis 1.'),
('John 1:14','plain_language','''The Word became flesh and dwelt among us'' can be pictured as ''pitched his tent among us.'' The Greek verb can carry the idea of living in a tent. John''s language echoes God''s presence among Israel, without suggesting Jesus literally lived in a tent.','John 1:14; Greek σκηνόω; Exodus 40:34–38.'),
('John 8:1','scholarly_debate','Why do some Bibles put brackets around the story of the woman accused of adultery? Many of the earliest Greek manuscripts of John do not contain John 7:53–8:11, although the story became well known in Christian tradition. Scholars discuss its textual history and where it first circulated.','John 7:53–8:11; critical Greek New Testament apparatus; compare modern Bible translation footnotes.'),
('John 8:7','plain_language','''Let him who is without sin throw the first stone'' came from a scene about a specific accusation and a proposed execution. The phrase is not a rule that nobody may ever correct wrongdoing; it directly challenges the accusers in that encounter.','John 8:1–11; textual status of the passage is debated; read in its narrative setting.'),
('Mark 16:9','scholarly_debate','Why do some Bible editions mark Mark 16:9–20 with a note? Two important early Greek manuscripts end at 16:8, while other manuscripts contain the longer ending. Many scholars think the longer ending was added later; traditions differ in how they use it.','Mark 16:8–20; Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus; modern critical apparatus and translation footnotes.'),
('Mark 16:8','scholarly_agreement','In the earliest form preserved by some major manuscripts, Mark ends with the women fleeing the tomb in fear. That''s a startling ending for a Gospel! The question is whether the longer ending was original, not whether Mark''s ending has a known manuscript history.','Mark 16:8–20; manuscript evidence Codex Sinaiticus and Vaticanus.'),
('Acts 2:1','original_language','''Pentecost'' comes from the Greek word for ''fiftieth.'' The festival was celebrated fifty days in connection with the preceding spring observances. It was already a Jewish feast when the Holy Spirit came in Acts—Christians did not invent the day from nothing.','Acts 2:1; Greek πεντηκοστή; Leviticus 23:15–16.'),
('Acts 2:5','ancient_culture','Jerusalem had visitors from many places during the festival, so Acts lists a crowd with different native languages. A striking part of the miracle is that ordinary people heard the message in languages they recognized from home.','Acts 2:5–12; Jewish pilgrimage festival setting.'),
('Deuteronomy 34:5','scholarly_debate','If Moses died in this chapter, who wrote the account of his death? Jewish and Christian interpreters have proposed Joshua or later inspired editing; modern scholarship also discusses how the Torah developed over time. The chapter itself does not name its writer.','Deuteronomy 34:5–12; history of Pentateuch interpretation and composition.'),
('Deuteronomy 34:10','plain_language','The line that no prophet ''has arisen'' like Moses looks backward from a later point in Israel''s story. It is one reason scholars discuss who wrote or edited the closing chapter of Deuteronomy.','Deuteronomy 34:10–12; literary perspective and Pentateuch composition debate.'),
('Psalms 119:1','original_language','Psalm 119 was arranged like a Hebrew alphabet poem: groups of eight verses begin with the same Hebrew letter, then move to the next letter. Imagine a huge A-to-Z poem, except the original alphabet had twenty-two letters.','Psalms 119; Hebrew acrostic, 22 stanzas x 8 verses.'),
('Psalms 119:9','ancient_culture','This psalm was designed so repeated letters and rhythm could help people remember it. Its alphabet pattern is mostly invisible in English, but the original Hebrew structure helps explain why the chapter feels so carefully arranged.','Psalms 119; Hebrew acrostic, the Beth stanza begins at verse 9.'),
('Genesis 1:1','scholarly_debate','Does the first Hebrew sentence mean ''In the beginning God created'' or ''When God began to create''? Translators debate its grammar. Both readings place God at the start of the creation account, but the opening sentence can be understood in more than one way.','Genesis 1:1–3; Hebrew bereshit grammatical discussion; compare translation notes.'),
('Genesis 1:3','ancient_culture','Genesis describes God''s creative word bringing light before it mentions the sun and moon on day four. Ancient readers weren''t being given a modern physics timetable; the text is making a carefully ordered statement about creation under God''s command.','Genesis 1:3–5,14–19; literary and theological structure; chronological interpretations differ.'),
('Genesis 2:4','original_language','Genesis repeats a Hebrew word often translated ''generations'' or ''account''—toledot—to introduce important sections. Here it helps the reader recognize a new movement in the story rather than a completely unrelated book.','Genesis 2:4; Hebrew תּוֹלְדוֹת; recurring formula in Genesis 5:1, 6:9 and elsewhere.'),
('Genesis 3:1','original_language','The Hebrew for ''naked'' near the end of Genesis 2 sounds strikingly similar to the word describing the serpent as ''crafty'' at the start of Genesis 3. The sound play links innocent vulnerability with the creature about to deceive.','Genesis 2:25–3:1; Hebrew arummim / arum soundplay.'),
('1 Corinthians 11:23','plain_language','When Paul says he ''delivered'' what he had ''received,'' think of faithfully passing on a teaching, not delivering a parcel. Scholars see this as an early Christian tradition about the Lord''s Supper, handed on before Paul''s letter was written.','1 Corinthians 11:23–26; Greek παραλαμβάνω / παραδίδωμι, transmission of tradition.'),
('1 Corinthians 11:25','ancient_culture','A ''covenant'' was more than a modern casual promise. In the biblical world it could establish a binding relationship with responsibilities. Jesus'' words about a ''new covenant'' echo Jeremiah''s promise of renewed relationship.','1 Corinthians 11:25; Jeremiah 31:31–34; covenant terminology.'),
('Revelation 1:4','scholarly_agreement','The seven churches named in Revelation were in real cities of Roman Asia, in what is now western Turkey. Their locations can be studied on maps and archaeological sites. That confirms the geographical setting, not every prophetic interpretation of the visions.','Revelation 1:4,11; sites including Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea.'),
('Revelation 1:11','plain_language','''Asia'' here does not mean the whole modern continent. It refers to the Roman province of Asia, much of which lay in what is now western Turkey. The seven letters had actual congregations and cities in view.','Revelation 1:11; Roman provincial geography.'),
('Revelation 22:14','scholarly_debate','Some translations say ''Blessed are those who wash their robes''; others say ''Blessed are those who do his commandments.'' This is a real manuscript difference. Scholars compare the ancient Greek copies to decide which wording was earlier.','Revelation 22:14; Greek textual variants, modern critical-text footnotes.'),
('Revelation 22:17','textual_connection','Near the end of the Bible, the Spirit and the Bride say ''Come,'' and the thirsty are invited to drink the water of life. This connects with Jesus'' earlier invitations to thirsty people in John 4 and John 7.','Revelation 22:17; John 4:10–14; John 7:37–39.'),
('Exodus 1:11','scholarly_debate','Exodus names the store-cities Pithom and Rameses. Archaeologists know Egyptian settlements associated with these names, but debate how best to identify the exact locations and what they imply for the date of the Exodus. A known name is not proof of every event.','Exodus 1:11; Egyptian Delta archaeology and contested Exodus chronology.'),
('Exodus 1:14','ancient_culture','The hard work described in Exodus includes making bricks and working in fields. Ancient Egyptian art and documents show brickmaking and organized labor were real activities, although they do not identify the workers in this verse by name.','Exodus 1:11–14; Egyptian New Kingdom brickmaking depictions.'),
('Matthew 1:17','scholarly_debate','Matthew arranges his genealogy into three groups of fourteen generations. Scholars discuss how the list is structured and why it differs from Luke''s genealogy. Ancient genealogies often selected names to communicate family identity, not always every generation in between.','Matthew 1:1–17; Luke 3:23–38; genealogy composition debates.'),
('Matthew 1:1','ancient_culture','Calling Jesus ''son of David'' did not mean David was His immediate father. In biblical genealogies, ''son of'' could mean a descendant many generations later. Matthew starts by introducing Jesus through covenant and royal family history.','Matthew 1:1,17; ancient genealogical idiom.'),
('Ruth 4:7','plain_language','Removing a sandal to confirm a land transaction sounds strange today. Think of it as a public, visible sign that a legal agreement was settled before witnesses. Ruth actually explains the custom because later readers might not know it.','Ruth 4:7–8; ancient Israelite public legal customs.'),
('Ruth 4:9','ancient_culture','The town gate could function like a public courthouse or council meeting place. Boaz gathers elders and witnesses there to settle the land and family matter in the open, rather than in a private conversation.','Ruth 4:1–12; ancient city-gate legal practice.')
